# Infrastructure Tycoon

Infrastructure Tycoon is a PC-first 2D business simulation targeting Unity and C#. The player grows a one-PC hosting business into a global cloud provider.

## MVP Interaction and Starter Scenario

Walk to the PC station to accept offers and assign workloads. Walk to the hardware station to buy upgrades and install equipment. Keep finances, time controls, and alerts visible in the HUD.

Use provisional balance values for the first playtest. Start with $150 cash and a PC with 2 CPU cores, 4 GB RAM, 250 GB storage, and 10 Mbps bandwidth. PhotoSnap pays $100 monthly for 1 core, 2 GB RAM, 20 GB storage, and 4 Mbps. A second website pays $80 monthly for 1 core, 1 GB RAM, 10 GB storage, and 4 Mbps. After two paid months PhotoSnap grows to 2 cores, triggering a CPU alert if both sites share the PC. A $120 upgrade adds 2 cores. Test the choice between buying headroom and saving toward the tower.

Unlock the $400 tower at $300 cumulative operating profit. Count hosting revenue less recurring expenses toward operating profit. Use $20 monthly expenses for the starter PC. Hardware purchases reduce available cash. Playtest the time to the first upgrade and tower with either one or both contracts.

For Phase 3, use these provisional one-time upgrades for the starter PC. Each upgrade can be purchased once per PC; none changes the $20 monthly PC expense. Purchases reduce cash, not cumulative operating profit. The CPU value retains the starter scenario above; RAM, storage, and internet values are new provisional playtest values.

| Upgrade | Price | Capacity added |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CPU | $120 | 2 cores |
| RAM | $80 | 4 GB |
| Storage | $100 | 250 GB |
| Internet | $90 | 10 Mbps |

PhotoSnap's first milestone becomes eligible after its second paid month. Its grown workload requires 2 CPU cores, 2 GB RAM, 20 GB storage, and 4 Mbps, still paying $100 monthly. With both starter sites hosted this raises combined CPU demand to 3 cores; the CPU upgrade provides 4 cores of capacity. Upgrade and milestone definitions are authored content; runtime purchase and growth behavior are tracked separately in SPEC.

Phase 3 verification on November 13, 2026 confirmed these provisional values in Unity 6000.6.9f1. Hosting both sites yields $160 operating profit per month: after two payments cash is $470 and cumulative operating profit $320. Buying CPU leaves $350 cash and $320 profit, changes usage from 3/2 to 3/4 cores, and clears the alert. The next month leaves $510 cash and $480 profit. Buying CPU at company creation leaves $30; RAM at $80 is rejected without changing cash, capacity, or purchase records. Save/load and legacy version 1 migration preserve this arithmetic without repeated payments, growth, or charges. Values were not retuned: this verifies accounting and interactions, while pacing and the choice to save for a tower remain playtest questions. The planned $300 profit unlock threshold is reached after two payments, but tower unlock/purchase behavior remains Phase 4 scope. Garage, player, and station visuals remain prototype art; overloads currently have no revenue or satisfaction penalty.

# Hardware & Infrastructure Progression Tree

This system governs the player's physical growth from a hobbyist hosting websites on a personal computer to operating global hyperscale cloud infrastructure.

The hardware progression should feel tangible and visually rewarding. Every upgrade should represent a meaningful milestone in the company's evolution.

---

# Design Goals

Hardware should:

- Increase available capacity
- Unlock new contract types
- Introduce new management challenges
- Create visible progression
- Encourage strategic upgrades

Players should constantly decide whether to:

- Upgrade existing equipment
- Buy additional equipment
- Expand facilities
- Replace aging hardware

---

# Era 1: Personal Computer

## Starter PC

Starting Equipment:

- 2 CPU Cores
- 4 GB RAM
- 250 GB Storage
- Consumer Internet

Can Host:

- Personal websites
- Blogs
- Portfolios

Limitations:

- Very low capacity
- No redundancy
- Frequent performance bottlenecks

Upgradeable Components:

- CPU
- RAM
- HDD/SSD
- Internet connection

